## Impact

The server returns the names and entry types of a directory outside its allowed directories to the MCP client, contrary to the README ("The server may touch only its **allowed directories**"; "All filesystem operations are restricted to allowed directories"). File contents and metadata are not returned. Where the server's user can list directories that the people able to write the allowed tree cannot, this also crosses an OS-user boundary, as demonstrated. The attack is a race and needs repeated calls.

Suggested CVSS 3.1: `CVSS:3.1/AV:L/AC:H/PR:L/UI:R/S:U/C:L/I:N/A:N` (2.2).

## Suggested fix

The patch below, against `e1dc4b18`, reads each listing through the anchor the reads already use:

- `readDirectoryInPlace(directory)` in `pinned-directory.ts` enters the validated directory with `inDirectory`, whose `getcwd` check refuses a swapped one, and reads `.`. Errors keep `fs.readdir`'s wording (`ENOENT: no such file or directory, scandir '<path>'`, `ENOTDIR: …`).
- The three `server.ts` call sites and `searchFilesWithValidation` use it. `search_files` now recurses on the path `validatePath` returned, while still reporting the requested path.
- `directory_tree` and `search_files` keep `readdirWithTimeout` as a bounded first read, so a stalled provider folder still times out (#4162) before the synchronous listing runs.
- New `__tests__/list-anchored.test.ts` stages the swap deterministically, as `read-anchored.test.ts` does, and swaps back before the next path under it is validated, as the racer does. Its six swap cases fail on `e1dc4b18` and pass with the patch; its eight unswapped cases pass on both. `bounded-walk.test.ts` and `lib.test.ts` give the anchored listing the same synthetic entries they give `readdir`.

With the patch, on Node 24.22.3, the filesystem suite passes (908 passed, 21 skipped), as do typecheck, lint, the format check and the per-file coverage gate. The harness above, run against a package packed from the patched tree, returned the outside name in 0 of 20,000 raced calls per tool, in two runs. In a further 5,000 raced calls per tool, between 271 (`search_files`) and 617 (`list_directory_with_sizes`) were refused with "a directory on the path changed during the operation".

This was run on Linux only. On Windows the working directory is a string, so the listing is no more anchored there than before, as the module header says of reads. Two behavior changes: the walkers read each directory twice, and a directory the server can read but not enter (`r` without `x`) is now refused with `EACCES`.

